@@ -69,6 +69,27 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Rich can pretty print a JSON document supplied as text with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json`. The output is the input re-serialized as multi-line JSON, and is highlighted by default. The ``indent`` argument sets the number of spaces to indent (defaults to 4), and ``highlight`` enables or disables syntax highlighting (defaults to True). Invalid JSON raises the parser's error and nothing is printed::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The same output is available from a :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable, which you can pass to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`. The renderable disables word wrapping, so default printing crops long lines to the console width. Use ``soft_wrap=True`` when printing if you want long lines kept whole::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.print(JSON('{"name": "rich"}'), soft_wrap=True)
+
+Rich also provides a package-level ``print_json`` function which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('[1, 2, 3]')
+
+To pretty print a JSON file from the command line, run the json module with the path to the file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
